    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The transpose of a matrix is a new matrix that is obtained by exchanging the rows and columns."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Let's see a figure to understand matrix transformation."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Matrix Transposition",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In NumPy, we can obtain the transpose of a matrix using the np.transpose() function. For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "1import numpy as np\n2\n3# create a matrix\n4matrix1 = np.array([[1, 2], \n5             \t      [3, 6]])\n6\n7# get transpose of matrix1\n8result = np.transpose(matrix1)\n9\n10print(result)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "[[1 3]\n [2 6]]"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now let's learn about what an identity matrix is."
